Part of #1065. **Builds on #1068, so merge that first.** This branch contains #1068's two commits; once #1068 is merged, only the commits below remain in the diff.

## Problem
After #1068, FilmCraft decodes DNG / CinemaDNG, but:
- every frame develops one fixed way, and a Raw settings panel has no struct to work with;
- lossy JPEG DNGs (Compression 34892) are refused;
- opcode lists (lens-shading gain maps, bad-pixel maps, vignette and distortion corrections, trims) are ignored (they stay ignored by default after this PR; see the opt-in below);
- a 4K 12-bit frame takes about 45–100 ms on 16 threads and 190 ms on one, which is too slow for smooth 4K playback;
- small monitors and proxies still develop at full size.

## Spec basis
- Adobe DNG Specification 1.7.1.0 (Sept. 2023), used together with 1.4–1.7:
  - Compression 34892 (allowed only for 8-bit LinearRaw);
  - chapter 5 (mapping to linear reference values);
  - chapter 7 (opcode list processing: the list format, flags, the 14 opcodes, the WarpRectilinear2 skip rule).
- ITU-T T.81 for the lossless-JPEG decoder.
- Krystek (1985), for the Planckian locus used by the temperature/tint white balance.
- No other decoder's source was read. Baseline JPEG is decoded by the `image` crate (jpeg feature only), which the workspace already uses in `codecs` and `media`. `Cargo.lock` only gains the `image` edge under `filmcraft-dng`; no new crate.

## Fix (one commit each)
1. **`ProcessOptions`** (`Default` gives exactly today's output). Entry points: `Dng::develop_with` and `decode_with`. Fields:
   - white balance: `AsShot`, or `Custom { temperature, tint }`. Tint follows the usual raw-converter sign: positive is more magenta, and D65 reads about +10 (fixed in a follow-up commit). `Dng::as_shot_temperature_tint` returns the as-shot values.
   - exposure in stops;
   - highlights: `Clip`, or `Recover` (documented as not implemented yet: it currently develops like `Clip`);
   - demosaic: `Fast` (bilinear) or `High` (Malvar);
  …

Fixes #1583.

## Problem

Portrait phone photos (a JPEG stored landscape with EXIF `Orientation = 6`) imported at their stored size and showed sideways. `decode_raster` decoded JPEG, PNG, WebP and TIFF stills with `image::load_from_memory`, which never applies the orientation tag (image 0.25).

## Fix

In the `image` branch of `decode_raster` (`crates/media/src/still.rs`):

- the orientation is read from the headers alone (`ImageReader` → `into_decoder` → `ImageDecoder::orientation()`): no pixels are decoded, and the probe's copy of the file is dropped before the real decode;
- pixels still go through `load_from_memory`, so its 512 MiB allocation limit keeps rejecting hostile headers (decoding through `into_decoder` + `from_decoder` would skip that check);
- `DynamicImage::apply_orientation` turns the picture before the RGBA conversion, and width/height are read afterwards.

`MediaInfo`, the frame, the auto anchor, Scale to Frame Size, thumbnails and image sequences all take their size from this decode, so they see the upright picture, the same way rotated phone video already works (pixels turned in the decoder, swapped size in `MediaInfo`). A missing, unknown (0, 9+) or damaged tag means no transform, never a failed import.

## Tests

New `crates/media/tests/still_orientation.rs` (11 tests, synthetic in-test fixtures):

- every orientation 1–8 in JPEG (APP1, little- and big-endian), PNG (`eXIf`), WebP (EXIF chunk) and TIFF (tag 274): output size and the position of each colour block checked against the EXIF definition, written independently of the `image` crate's transform;
- `open_bytes`: `info().video` equals the upright size and the frame size;
- image sequences, also with frames of different orientations;
- unchanged pictures for no EXIF, values 0/9/255/65535, wrong tag type/count, truncated EXIF, and YCbCr TIFF (out of scope, see below);
- mutation fuzz (every truncation, bit flips) of oriented JPEG/PNG/WebP/TIFF files under `catch_unwind`, and a 65535×65535 h…

Part of the audit described in the companion issue (findings, reachability arguments, and patches for the remaining defects are filed there).

## What this fixes

FilmCraft: minimal fix with a regression test, generated from the audit at `cee1feb` and applied on top of current `main`.

```diff
--- a/crates/interchange/src/common.rs
+++ b/crates/interchange/src/common.rs
@@ -65,7 +65,8 @@
         return url.to_string();
     }
     let rest = &url[7..];
-    let rest = if rest.len() >= 9 && rest[..9].eq_ignore_ascii_case("localhost") { &rest[9..] } else { rest };
+    // Through `get`: `rest[..9]` would panic when byte 9 is inside a multi-byte character.
+    let rest = if rest.get(..9).is_some_and(|p| p.eq_ignore_ascii_case("localhost")) { &rest[9..] } else { rest };
     let decoded = percent_decode(rest);
     // file:///C:/x -> C:/x
     if decoded.len() >= 3 && decoded.starts_with('/') && is_windows_abs(&decoded[1..]) {
@@ -692,7 +693,15 @@
         }
     }

+    /// A `file://` URL whose ninth byte is inside a multi-byte character (a hostile import): the
+    /// `localhost` prefix check must not slice the character in half.
     #[test]
+    fn file_url_with_a_multibyte_character_at_the_localhost_offset_does_not_panic() {
+        assert_eq!(file_url_to_path("file://12345678é"), "//12345678é");
+        assert_eq!(file_url_to_path("file://LOCALHOST/Users/me/a.mov"), "/Users/me/a.mov");
+    }
+
+    #[test]
     fn paths() {
         assert_eq!(resolve_path("media/a.mov", Some("/proj")), "/proj/media/a.mov");
         assert_eq!(resolve_path("../a.mov", Some("/proj/x/")), "/proj/a.mov");
```

## Verification

Regression test included (`file_url_with_a_multibyte_character_at_the_localhost_offset_does_not_panic`), verified locally:

```sh
$ cargo test -p filmcraft-interchange --lib file_url_with_a_multibyte
test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed
```

Per CONTRIBUTING.md this follows the Never-crash rule; `cargo xtask ci` should confirm the full gate.

