Found while reviewing #2247, which fixes the same shape in `parse_number_core`: a `break` that stops consuming the digit run, so the tokenizer resumes in the middle of a number. `parse_amount` has the same defect (no existing issue).

## Cause

`parse_amount` stops collecting integer digits at the cap and, in doing so, stops consuming them:

```rust
} else if int_digits.len() > MAX_CARDINAL_DIGITS {
    break;
}
used = i + 1;
```

`used` is what the caller consumes, and the `break` skips the update for the remainder of the run. `parse_money` then takes its oversized branch on the digits it can see:

```rust
if digits > MAX_CARDINAL_DIGITS || has_leading_zero(&value, digits) {
    // Oversized amounts: every digit, unit-free, no overflow.
    let all = rest.get(skip..skip + used)?.chars().filter(|c| c.is_ascii_digit()).collect::<String>();
```

So the first 13 digits are spoken digit by digit and the rest of the amount goes back through the normal number path. A leftover of at most `MAX_CARDINAL_DIGITS` digits without a leading zero is spoken as a cardinal. With the crate's own `words()` helper, 25 digits with and without the symbol:

```
SEM  $ 1234567890123456789012345 -> one two three four five six seven eight nine zero one two three four five six seven eight nine zero one two three four five
COM  $ 1234567890123456789012345 -> one two three four five six seven eight nine zero one two three four hundred fifty-six billion seven hundred eighty-nine million twelve thousand three hundred forty-five
```

The window is 15 to 25 digits. At 14 the leftover is a single digit, so the words happen to come out right; past 25 the leftover is longer than the cap and takes the digit-by-digit path again. Below 15 the cap is never reached.

## Fix

The cap is not the bug: it exists so `int_digits` stays parseable as a `u64`. Keep the cap and consume the run:

```rust
} else if int_digits.len() <= MAX_CARDINAL_DIGITS {
    int_digits.push(ch);
}
```

